 "script": [
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "In eighteen thirty-five, a New York newspaper told the whole city there were bat-people living on the Moon. And people bought it."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "Not a cartoon. A real front page. They called it the Great Moon Hoax."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Here's the deal. The New York Sun said a famous astronomer, Sir John Herschel, had built a giant telescope in South Africa."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "With a lens twenty-four feet wide, it could see the Moon like it was right down the street."
  },
  {
   "beat": "moon",
   "text": "And what did he see? Blue unicorns. Beavers walking on two legs. Giant purple crystals."
  },
  {
   "beat": "moon",
   "text": "And then, the big one. Furry little people, with bat wings, flying over the lakes."
  },
  {
   "beat": "boom",
   "text": "New Yorkers went wild. The Sun claimed it was the best-selling paper in the world."
  },
  {
   "beat": "boom",
   "text": "Other papers copied the story word for word, just to keep up."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "And you know who had no idea? Herschel himself. He was real, and he really was in South Africa. He just never saw a single bat-man."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "Turns out a reporter made the whole thing up. Most likely a guy named Richard Adams Locke."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "And when the truth came out? The Sun never really said sorry. And sales stayed sky high."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "So next time something online sounds too wild to be true, check the telescope."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "That's real. That's about it, folks. Follow for more strange history. See ya!"
  }
 ],
 "science": [
  {
   "claim": "In August 1835 the New York Sun ran six articles claiming Sir John Herschel, then really observing at the Cape of Good Hope, had seen life on the Moon through a giant telescope (a lens said to be 24 feet across): bison-like animals, blue unicorn-like goats, biped beavers, huge amethyst-like crystals and winged 'man-bats' (Vespertilio-homo). Other papers reprinted it; the Sun claimed the largest circulation in the world (about 19,000). The author is generally believed to be reporter Richard Adams Locke. The Sun never formally retracted it.",
   "working": "",
   "source": "Wikipedia, Great Moon Hoax; Smithsonian; Library of Congress; Museum of Hoaxes"
  }
 ],
